# What NOT to send to Cloud Build.
#
# This file has to exist, and the reason is a trap rather than an optimisation.
# When there is no .gcloudignore, `gcloud run deploy --source .` falls back to
# reading .gitignore -- and .gitignore excludes data/kb.json, deliberately,
# because a 900 KB knowledge base does not belong in a git repo. It very much
# does belong in the image. Without this file the build silently succeeds and
# the deployed assistant comes up with an empty library, citing nothing, which
# reads as a broken bot rather than an unpopulated one. (/api/kb reporting
# "docs: 0" is that failure.)
#
# So: list the exclusions explicitly here, and data/ is conspicuously not among
# them.

# Several hundred megabytes of Windows virtualenv that the image rebuilds from
# requirements.txt anyway. This is the one that actually matters -- uploading it
# is slow and it is useless once uploaded, because Linux cannot run it.
.venv/
venv/

# Secrets. Cloud Run reads environment variables set with `gcloud run services
# update`, never a file. A .env inside a container image is a .env you have
# published.
.env

# Compiled bytecode from a different Python on a different OS.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/

# Training inputs for the pocket calibration, ~7 MB of STEP. The calibration
# result (data/pocket_cal.json) is what the app reads; the parts it was derived
# from are not needed at runtime.
reference_parts/

# Written at run time.
outputs/

# Windows launchers and local history -- nothing the container can use.
*.bat
.git/
.gitignore
stressviz-cloud.zip
